WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.861 --> 00:00:01.694
- I love your intro though,

00:00:01.694 --> 00:00:02.949
turning around with a computer.

00:00:02.949 --> 00:00:04.167
- I know, right. now.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:05.000
Hello.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:05.833
- Well, hello there.

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:08.250
(calm music)

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.140
So welcome everyone.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.900
My name is Terrell Gibson and I'm here to

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.930
welcome my friend, my colleague

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.550
and now my peer in the Academy,

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.960
Shannon Baker Davis.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.410
- Hello, I am Shannon Baker Davis.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.060
I'm a picture editor.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.770
Yeah, I'm just happy to be here.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.050
- I mean, I know you've
come up in a similar way

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.120
that I came up, which is like old school

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.490
there were no shortcuts whatsoever.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:45.960
You know, we started
from the very beginning

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.930
and built a body of work and
got to a certain position

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.030
and then had to kind of
reboot and start over

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.330
and work on some smaller projects again.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.640
Cut your rate in half, try to build it up

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.410
and you're constantly in that reset.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.320
And then you get to a
moment like this, right,

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.208
where you realize decades later

00:01:05.208 --> 00:01:06.570
that all of that has kind
of like come to fruition

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.304
and really paid off.

00:01:08.304 --> 00:01:09.900
So it's really good to
kind of like step back

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.600
and outside of yourself every
now and then and just realize,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.260
wow this was an incredible journey.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.240
This is an incredible journey.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.010
I certainly as a kid from New Jersey

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.190
with no connections to the
film business whatsoever,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.728
didn't have that sort of,

00:01:25.728 --> 00:01:28.560
I guess you would say just that,

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.998
they say you have to see it to be it.

00:01:30.998 --> 00:01:32.970
I never saw it, you know what I mean?

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.470
Started working in New York,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.760
never ran into another
person that looked like me

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.333
in that period of time.

00:01:40.702 --> 00:01:42.030
But slowly but surely you realize

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.253
we all are out there, you know?

00:01:45.096 --> 00:01:45.929
- Yeah, I'm the same way.

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:47.250
I grew up in Augusta, Georgia.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.290
My mom is a nuclear chemist

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.210
and my dad as an electrical engineer

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.400
and I was like, I wanna do movies.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.940
And they're like, well,
how are you gonna do that?

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.890
Like we don't know anything about that.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.648
You can't do that.

00:02:00.648 --> 00:02:03.270
So you have to have an extra bit of drive

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.960
when you don't have anybody in your world

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.320
that is experienced working up the ranks

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.320
or been handed a job or has a relative,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.150
a parent that already
works in the industry.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:23.150
And I'm still amazed that I
get to do this for a career

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.040
and I get to do this to make money

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.040
and like no matter like
when I am up till 2:00 AM

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:37.550
or I've gotten another round
of notes and I'm complaining,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.310
like the moment that someone
says well what do you do

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.746
and you tell them and then they're like,

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:45.210
oh my God, that's so amazing.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.990
And you're like, I guess
it is kind of amazing,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.610
and it just makes you remember

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.250
that you were that person that just

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.530
like glamorized this whole business.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.550
And no matter how it
may trouble you at times

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:04.020
you still, I still I'm
so proud to be an editor

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.060
and proud to work on feature films

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.100
and work with some of the people

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.870
that I've worked with over the years,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.213
like it's still amazing to me.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.787
- So I kept running back in my mind

00:03:14.787 --> 00:03:18.603
how you might respond to me
asking you for a drink after.

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:21.833
- So that was you asking me?

00:03:22.825 --> 00:03:23.850
That was elaborate.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:24.683
- In my mind it wasn't that forward,

00:03:24.683 --> 00:03:25.976
you know what I mean?

00:03:25.976 --> 00:03:27.660
It was more smoother than that.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.883
- I was supposed to call you I thought.

00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:30.923
- Yeah, you were supposed to,

00:03:32.672 --> 00:03:33.844
but you didn't.

00:03:33.844 --> 00:03:34.813
- So yeah, talk to us a little bit more

00:03:34.813 --> 00:03:37.290
about that in terms of your arc
in terms of starting reality

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:40.180
and then getting into
scripted and then featured.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.290
- Yeah, so I graduated from AFI in 2003

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.410
and there were not a lot of jobs

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.940
in scripted when I came out.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.560
There weren't, there was no Netflix,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.483
there were no streaming platforms,

00:03:52.578 --> 00:03:53.700
it was like you worked on Law and Order

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.700
or you worked on some sitcom.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.560
And so I moved back to the East coast

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.490
and got a job on a HGTV show

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.490
and I was happy to assist,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.040
but I always knew I wanted to cut.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.410
And so I just kept with that

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:14.760
because I had narrative like education.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.830
I had a narrative filmmaking education

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.350
and that helped me a lot in reality TV

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.830
because we were constructing the story.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.750
We were taking footage that was

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.300
and it was similar to
documentary filmmaking

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.760
where they shot over a long period of time

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.610
and the editors had to figure
out what the story was.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:36.420
I did that for 10 years and
worked on shows that won Emmy's,

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.320
but I decided I really
wanted to do narrative

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.450
and so I moved back to LA.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.630
Two friends were directing
an independent feature

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.940
and we had been talking about
me at editing it for years

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.280
and they finally got the money
together and they were like

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.250
we understand if you don't feel ready,

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.080
but I was like this is now or never.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.080
So I cut that independent
feature and loved it so much

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.230
and I was like, this is it,

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.940
this is what I have to do.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.280
Networking and trying to, you know,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.077
and sending out cold
emails and doing a lot,

00:05:09.077 --> 00:05:11.280
meeting with people and Katie Skirping

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.200
who was on the Good Wife

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.960
and she had been the
assistant on the Good Wife

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.970
for six seasons and
she was being bumped up

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.160
for the seventh season, the last season.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.620
And she was like, I feel like I,

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.210
she's like I can't hire you now,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:27.780
they won't let me hire someone so green

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.183
because I am a new editor.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:32.123
She's like, but let's stay in touch.

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:33.660
And we stayed in touch.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.410
And then at one point during the season

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.050
the assistant editor that she had hired

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.540
had to leave and she said,

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.940
she called me on like a
Friday and she was like,

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:45.960
can you come on Monday?

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.797
And I said, I'm there.

00:05:47.797 --> 00:05:49.440
And it worked out like
I assisted that show

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.480
and then I assisted Jamie Nelson

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.370
on the first season of Insecure.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.592
Then I assisted Ferrell Levy,

00:05:55.592 --> 00:05:57.540
who was my professor at AFI.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.340
And then I talked my way into

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.683
an editing job on a new series for BET.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:09.840
And all the while I was
cutting independent features,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.140
cutting shorts, just
cutting anything I could

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.760
that was narrative.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.564
That's the way I got to
cut The Weekend with Stella

00:06:19.564 --> 00:06:21.180
and that just like catapulted my career

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.381
cuz we got into Toronto
and it was in the theater

00:06:25.381 --> 00:06:29.763
and then we did The Photograph together.

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:34.080
And once you do a film that either does

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.670
really well at the festivals
or gets picked up by a studio,

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:40.230
gets picked up by a streamer,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:45.230
it's like that is then you
have your name is on lists

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.513
and it just becomes a little bit easier.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.343
- Oh yeah, Spin Doctors, classic.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:55.413
Tim, I wanna chop it up more,

00:06:55.413 --> 00:06:57.453
but I gotta get to my squash game.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.183
Was that Visa or MasterCard?

00:07:07.535 --> 00:07:09.301
(hands slappings)

00:07:09.301 --> 00:07:11.884
(upbeat music)

00:07:14.668 --> 00:07:17.910
- There's an element of
persistence when you're an editor.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.280
I would say that about being an editor

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.990
you can't be a quitter.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.930
You have to be the one person that's like

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.730
everybody quit around me,

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.770
but I'm gonna go sit in my avid

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:30.450
and I'm gonna get this thing done.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.490
Like I'm gonna be that person.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.220
And I would just encourage,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.070
if that's not how you
feel about something,

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.980
then probably there's
something else for you.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:42.363
There's something else,

00:07:43.448 --> 00:07:44.998
there's something for everyone.

00:07:45.874 --> 00:07:47.430
But there are certain
aspects of a personality

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:51.270
that an editor just has to have and feel

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.343
and want to have in themselves.

00:07:55.093 --> 00:07:56.883
So that's why I say persistence.

00:07:58.041 --> 00:07:59.910
- Every day you get in to
ask yourself the question

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:03.229
when you leave did I make it better today?

00:08:03.229 --> 00:08:06.640
And then the accumulation
hopefully of months and months of

00:08:07.856 --> 00:08:09.090
really putting the screws to it,

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.800
hopefully you end up on the other side

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.440
with something that everybody's proud of,

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.981
so I'd say that and
particularly African-American

00:08:16.981 --> 00:08:18.676
young aspiring editors.

00:08:18.676 --> 00:08:21.720
Cuz when I came up I knew
of literally two maybe

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:22.860
and I might be,

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:23.853
that might be,

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.393
I might be padding that by one.

00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:29.430
But Jess, if you see the two of us

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.263
and you're watching this right now,

00:08:32.552 --> 00:08:34.440
understand that if we've made it this far,

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.823
certainly you can too.

00:08:37.069 --> 00:08:38.640
So keep working, keep working.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.146
- Of course.

00:08:40.146 --> 00:08:41.048
- Yeah.

00:08:41.048 --> 00:08:42.120
- And I always hope that
like we both talked about

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.760
how long it took to get to where we are

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.510
and I always hope that it'll
be easier for the next person.

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:51.090
I always hope that whatever I do

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.600
that I impressed enough
people that they'll be like,

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.950
oh we gave this young Black woman a shot,

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:02.269
I'm not gonna be so afraid to do it again.

00:09:02.269 --> 00:09:05.580
Really be able to feel free to
have an opinion about things

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.470
and walk into the room

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:09.933
and know that you know what you're doing.

00:09:10.909 --> 00:09:13.950
And yeah I always hope that
it'll be a little bit easier

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:18.950
and there'll be like three
or four people next year

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.540
or in a couple years
that are being invited

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.450
into the Academy that are people of color

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:27.903
or women or women of color.

